From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 08:05:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/13] REP MOVS/STOS: a fault part way through resumes at the
 iteration the loop reached

MemCpy and MemSet run the whole instruction as one loop over copies of RCX, RSI and RDI, so a fault inside it left the guest registers at the first iteration and a handler that continued (a page committed on demand, a guard page) restarted the copy from the start. For an overlapping copy (memmove) that reads source bytes the first pass already overwrote: 2047 of 4096 bytes came out wrong in the reproducer. x86 reports the faulting iteration and continues from there.

RestoreStringOpProgressFromHostPC recognises the loop's loads and stores at the fault PC (DecodeStringLoopAccess, next to the emitters), checks that the guest instruction is REP MOVS or STOS, and moves RCX, RSI and RDI to the element or 32-byte block the loop reached; the continued instruction redoes that block, which is harmless because the block loops only run when |dst - src| >= 32. ARM64EC's ReconstructThreadState applies it for guest exceptions and for inline self-modifying code. The fault address stays the host one, in the same page as the x86 one. MOPS forms are left alone.
